cl inlines every lambda in this case away and emits no symbol for any of them; cxx1 has no COMDAT and no way to say a definition is mergeable, so it emits a real function for each call operator. Every difference here is a name cxx1 has and cl has none of - the same difference local-class.nocl and docs/CONFORMANCE.md record as "an inline member function is a strong symbol".

Were cl to emit one it would still spell it differently, and all three parts were measured before any of this was written: cl calls a closure <lambda_0> where cxx1 calls it $_0; it writes the local-scope index ?0? where an ordinary local class gets ?1?; and it writes @ in place of the call operator's return type, where cxx1 writes the type. None of it is reachable from outside - a closure type is unnamed, [expr.prim.lambda], its call operator is reached through the object, and the object never leaves the function that made it - so there is nothing here for two objects to disagree about at a link. Recorded rather than matched, and tests/cases/lambda.nonames says the same about clang's numbering.
